A||||5||Tom88gt||junkyard t-tops||||5||
Z||000000||Tom88gt||01-04-2000||05:23 PM||||I just picked up a set of t-tops and seals off a junkyard 85SE for spare parts (the car is beyond economical restoration, straight-clean body but completely stripped).  I'm wondering if it's worth trying to remove the whole top section for additional t-top parts or to transplant to another car?  Any thoughts?||||reg||5
Z||000001||DRH||01-04-2000||05:36 PM||||Seems to me you might be able to find someone interested in a t-top "conversion kit". If you can get it cheap enough and have somewhere to put it you might even get it for your own future use on another car. Anybody know exactly what parts would be needed?||||reg||1
Z||000002||88 Database Nut||01-04-2000||05:37 PM||||I would get as much as you can, If I were you. There weren't that many kits made, and the ones available now are still fairly expensive, so that even if you got only a few parts I'd bet that they would be worth alot to someone who's looking for them in the years to come.  If you goto the TTop registry site (I believe it can be reached at fiero.net), they have a nice online book which shows the various parts involved, it might help the removal process.  But, since you already are familar with TTops, I'll bet you already knew about that site.<BR>Anyway, Good Luck, <BR>John<BR>Anyway, It'd be a chore, but it's gotta pay off someday, right?||||reg||14
Z||000003||mrfixit58||01-04-2000||05:41 PM||||I would try to get the chassis cheap before it goes to the crusher. Or, you could cut the entire top off the car. I'm not sure what was involved when the top was modified but it would be nice to have an original for reference or... even for resale to those of us unfortuate enough to NOT have a T-top. Also, be sure you get the gutter tray from under the t-tops. In gereral, get everything you can to convert an non- T-top car.<P>congrats on a great find!!! ||||reg||4
Z||000004||Tom88gt||01-04-2000||06:14 PM||||I've grabbed most of the easily removable parts that are t-top unique.  I'll probably never need them but you never know (my car could get stolen, or top wrecked).  I believe the entire top can be bolted off but is an extensive process.  If I had a place to put the car I'd buy the whole thing but I'll be moving in a couple of years (military) and can't afford to drag around a parts car/future restoration.  Is there a frame support bracket or something under the car on t-top models?  PISA's targa kit requires one but I'm not sure about the t-tops since the car has a support?? beam installed in the middle on the roof.||||reg||1
Z||000005||mrfiero||01-04-2000||08:08 PM||||If you cut the roof off make sure you don't destroy the door weather seal....these are VERY hard to come by.<P>The T-Top frame is riveted to the chassis, but some frame cutting is required to retrofit to another Fiero frame.  If the car with the T-Tops has a straight frame and good title it would be easier in the long run to rebuild it with parts from another Fiero.  <P>It's a very involved process to install a T-Top and if it's not done properly you can really screw up the car.<P>That said.....where is this car located?  If you have no interest in the frame I'd like to get it!<P>BTW...there is no lower frame support bracket on T-Top cars.  When installed, the T-Top frame was pretty much as rigid/strong as the regular roof, so no additional support was needed. ||||reg||1
